Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Rail Seven Refuses the Tuner
- Story & Context: At 156 BPM, Pimchanok repeats one mid-register ranat note while Andre holds the nearest 12-tone acid-bass pitch beneath it. The two sources seem almost aligned but produce a slow audible beat. Kick doubles enter around the pulse without synchronizing to it. After eight bars, Nicha adds one damped open-fifth Rhodes dyad and immediately removes it when the friction becomes masked. Somchai replaces every fourth clap with a dry klong khaek slap. The central section strips to ranat, acid bass, kick, and room tone so Near-Lock becomes the main event.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Rain-streaked signal board marked RAIL 7 beside a handwritten tuning chart with two nearly overlapping frequency lines, lacquered mallets resting on a chrome relay cabinet. Sparse lyric fragments: “rail seven,” “almost aligned,” “leave it sharp,” “tuner off.”

Track 2: Wet Detour at Signal 43
- Story & Context: At 160 BPM, the ranat maintains the same four-note pattern for the entire track while Andre shifts the acid-bass reference by one semitone during each Wet Detour. The acoustic melody never changes, but its perceived consonance changes sharply as the electronic note moves. Rail Flash passages cross the full ranat register while hats disappear completely. Renee enters during a four-bar Dry Return with low breath-only bass-flute attacks. When the acid bass re-enters, it lands on the least stable pairing of the EP.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Elevated-rail signal plate numbered 43 glowing through monsoon rain, two waveform traces diverging beneath it, one green route lamp reflected in wet steel. Sparse lyric fragments: “signal forty-three,” “wet detour,” “same wood,” “new voltage.”

Track 3: Chrome Bars After the Last Train
- Story & Context: The closer runs at 148 BPM and begins without acid bass. Pimchanok plays sparse ranat dyads over kick, clap, klong khaek, and bass-flute air. Andre introduces the 303 reference only after two minutes, first at very low level, then gradually brighter. Every return of the bass creates a new Near-Lock against a different ranat register. Nicha uses only three prepared-piano events in the entire piece. In the final minute, the drums thin until only one compact kick remains every two bars. Pimchanok sustains a tremolo on the closest ranat pitch while Andre holds a single acid note. The EP ends before either pitch resolves or is corrected.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Empty elevated-rail workshop after midnight, chrome relay bars under fluorescent light, a ranat ek facing a silent 303 sequencer, rainwater running beneath the open shutter. Sparse lyric fragments: “last train,” “chrome bars,” “still not equal,” “leave it there.”

